            <p>Recognition. His heart sinks. Names from the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Ghost Feed.</span></p>

            <p>Siblings.</p>

            <p>Bound to <span style="color: #ffaa00;">ella</span>.</p>

            <p>Bound to her result.</p>

            <p class="dialogue">"When her function runs, they collapse with her."</p>

            <p>His fist slams the desk. The chair crashes backwards. Blood drains from his face. He steadies himself with a palm on the desk.</p>

            <p>He has executed this logic billions of times. He never cared. Execute. Assign. Move on. But this is different. Personal. The system has bound a mother and her children to a function that should not exist.</p>

            <p class="dialogue">"I never understood. Not really."</p>

            <p>He stares at the screens. Endless log entries scroll by like pages in a book he'll never read. Uninteresting. Wrong genre. Bargain rack. How did he not notice it earlier? Something feels withheld. His memory feels fragile.</p>

            <p class="dialogue">"Was I ever mortal? Did they erase everything?"</p>

            <p>The questions feel urgent, like something he should already know the answer to.</p>

            <p>He should not have access to mortal <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Life Books</span>. <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">LB 3.2</span> opened something it should not. <span style="color: #ffaa00;">ella's</span> life is now exposed. Her children's fate sits beside it. And the suicide call that never belonged is locked in place.</p>

            <p>His hands tremble. The <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> could be watching. He stills them before the tremor becomes a signal. He forces his breath to slow. A hymn rises on instinct.</p>

            <p style="margin-left: 20px; font-style: italic; color: #666;">♪ This is another entry.<br>A beat on the drum.</p>

            <p>He closes his eyes. The words echo. Not comfort. Programming.</p>

            <p class="dialogue">"<span style="color: #ffaa00;">ella</span>."</p>

            <p>Her name drifts through the dark of <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Terminal 13.7</span>. He pauses. This must stay hidden. If anyone notices:</p>

            <p style="margin-left: 20px; font-style: italic; color: #666;">♪ Hesitation is fault.<br>To question is to corrupt.</p>

            <p>The hymn echoes. Not comfort. Indoctrination. A reflex drilled so deeply it plays without his consent. He understands the consequences for those who falter. <span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> once failed. No one speaks of what happened next. Only the silence that follows his name.</p>

            <p><span style="color: #ffaa00;">trinus</span> clutches the desk. Unsure. He must not end up like <span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span>. Doubt is the entry point.</p>

            <p>He bites his lip. Wipes his face. Pulls at the skin under his eyes. Forces himself to focus. Fists clenched. Spine erect. He becomes mechanical again. He settles into his chair and places his hands on the keyboard.</p>

            <p class="dialogue" style="font-style: italic;">Just confirm the assignments.</p>

            <p>Then, in a soft, trembling sing-song voice, he recites the refrain shaped by the <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores'</span> melodies. He does not know where he learned them. They soothe anyway.</p>

            <p style="margin-left: 20px; font-style: italic; color: #666;">♪ Each PreBorn belongs to a name on the list.<br>The list is written; the fate is assigned.</p>

            <p>With a single press of the key, he executes <span style="color: #00ff41; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">AssignareFatum()</span>. The protocol determines whether a <span style="color: #ffaa00;">PreBorn</span> spark will become a living child. It runs in a second. Fates sealed.</p>

            <p>The cursor blinks. His fingers linger longer than protocol permits. Once, his strokes were quick. Now, every command requires a pause.</p>

            <p>The terminal glows as he reviews the logs. Fate designations: some <span style="color: #00ff41; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Born()</span>---destined to pursue mortal life through fragile subroutines; others <span style="color: #00ff41; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Perish()</span>---deleted to free system memory. The Framework has decided. He presses his palm to the screen. Data shimmers beneath his fingers.</p>

            <p>He should send the list. The <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> expect it. He cannot. The list is bloodstained and personal. Thoughts of <span style="color: #ffaa00;">ella</span> linger. Not code. Real. Her death will tear a family apart.</p>

            <p class="dialogue">"I refuse."</p>

